{#social}
## Community & Family Impacts

On balance, *more Americans report negative impacts on their communities rather than positive ones*{.finding}.
Most Americans say they are seeing people in their communities struggling to tell what's real from fake because of AI.
Among all adults, {{.Share "seeing-fake" "008+009"}} say they are seeing it or are starting to.

A smaller majority are seeing people lose connections to others ({{.Share "seeing-connections" "008+009"}}).
About half are seeing people pressured to do more, faster ({{.Share "seeing-pressure" "008+009"}}).
On the positive side, just as many are seeing people save time on everyday tasks ({{.Share "seeing-time" "008+009"}}).
Just {{.Share "seeing-healthcare" "008+009"}} are seeing people get better health care.

{{chart "what-people-are-seeing"}}

*More Americans say AI is making things worse rather than better for American families*{.finding}.
The widest gap is on job security: {{.Share "worse-jobs" "008+009"}} of adults say AI is making it worse, compared to {{.Share "better-jobs" "008+009"}} who say better.
Most also say it is making children's well-being ({{.Share "worse-children" "008+009"}}) and families' outlook on the future ({{.Share "worse-outlook" "008+009"}}) worse.

The gap is smallest on household finances, where {{.Share "worse-finances" "008+009"}} say worse and {{.Share "better-finances" "008+009"}} better.
But {{.Share "no-effect-finances" "008+009"}} say it's having no effect, the most of the four.

{{chart "better-or-worse"}}

