{#futures}
## Future Scenarios

*Most Americans think consequential future scenarios involving AI are likely, or are already happening*{.finding}: from {{.Share "likely-breakthroughs" "008+009"}} for major scientific and medical breakthroughs to {{.Share "likely-unintended" "008+009"}} for losing control of AI systems.

{{chart "unlikely-or-likely"}}

What has changed is how many say some of these things are already happening.

{{chart "already-happening-over-time"}}

About a third now say AI is already smarter than humans at most tasks: {{.Share "already-smarter" "009"}} in {{.When "009"}}, up from {{.Share "already-smarter" "001"}} in {{.When "001"}} ({{.Change "already-smarter" "001" "009"}}).

*The largest rise is in loss of control: AI behaving in ways its developers did not intend*{.finding}.
The share who say it is already happening has climbed from {{.Share "already-unintended" "001"}} in {{.When "001"}} to {{.Share "already-unintended" "009"}} in {{.When "009"}} ({{.Change "already-unintended" "001" "009"}}).
At the same time, the share who say it is likely fell from {{.Share "likely-to-happen-unintended" "001"}} to {{.Share "likely-to-happen-unintended" "009"}} ({{.Change "likely-to-happen-unintended" "001" "009"}}).
The lines have crossed: in the three latest surveys, more Americans say it is already happening than say it is likely to in the future, {{.Share "already-unintended" "009"}} to {{.Share "likely-to-happen-unintended" "009"}} in {{.When "009"}}.

{{chart "loss-of-control-over-time"}}

