{#government}
## Government Action

*Most Americans say the government should make dealing with harms from artificial intelligence a priority*{.finding}.
Among all adults, {{.Share "any-ai-harms" "008+009"}} say it should be a priority, with {{.Share "top-ai-harms" "008+009"}} calling it a top priority and {{.Share "important-ai-harms" "008+009"}} an important but lower one.

Among the seven issues we track, AI harms ranks fifth: behind inflation and health care, then jobs and crime, and ahead of immigration and climate change.

{{chart "how-much-of-a-priority"}}

*The share of Americans calling AI harms a top priority rose after Jacob Coxon's resignation*{.finding}: from {{.Share "top-ai-harms" "005"}} in {{.When "005"}} to {{.Share "top-ai-harms" "006"}} in {{.When "006"}} and {{.Share "top-ai-harms" "007"}} in {{.When "007"}} ({{.Change "top-ai-harms" "005" "007"}}). It has since fallen back, to {{.Share "top-ai-harms" "008"}} in {{.When "008"}} and {{.Share "top-ai-harms" "009"}} in {{.When "009"}} ({{.Change "top-ai-harms" "007" "009"}}).

{{chart "ai-harms-over-time"}}

Wherever AI ranks among their priorities, *more Americans say the government is doing too little about it than too much*{.finding}.

On AI's effects on children and young people, {{.Share "little-children" "008+009"}} of adults say too little and {{.Share "much-children" "008+009"}} too much, and the split is similar on its environmental footprint and its effects on workers and jobs.

The gap is smallest on American competitiveness, where {{.Share "little-competitiveness" "008+009"}} say too little, {{.Share "much-competitiveness" "008+009"}} too much and {{.Share "unsure-competitiveness" "008+009"}} aren't sure.

{{chart "too-much-or-too-little"}}

